  "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4x).$ Then  we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}\u20614$ | $\\operatorname{ln}\u20614e$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4x)}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614e}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}|}_{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614e} \\\\  & ={\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {u}^{2}|}_{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614e} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}((\\operatorname{ln}\u20614e)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}\u20614)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
